Scale and Expose Applications to External Access
Objectives
In this section you learn how to:
1.	Allocate IP addresses for pods and services
2.	Enable communication between pods
3.	Expose applications outside the cluster
4.	Scale applications using multiple pods
________________________________________
1. Why Applications Need Scaling
Real applications receive many user requests.
Instead of running one pod, Kubernetes runs multiple identical pods.
Example:
Frontend Service
      |
   Load Balancer
   /    |     \
 Pod1  Pod2  Pod3
Benefits:
•	handles more traffic
•	improves reliability
•	allows horizontal scaling
________________________________________
2. Service IP Addresses
Each Kubernetes Service gets a stable IP address.
Important characteristics:
Feature	Description
Stable IP	does not change
Load balancing	distributes traffic
Service discovery	reachable by DNS
Service IP example:
172.30.108.92
This IP comes from the service network.
________________________________________
Pod Network vs Service Network
Network	Purpose
Pod Network	communication between pods
Service Network	stable IP for services
Example configuration:
Pod Network CIDR      → 10.128.0.0/14
Service Network CIDR  → 172.30.0.0/16
Networking inside the cluster is managed by:
•	OVN-Kubernetes
________________________________________
3. Why Pods Should Not Use Pod IPs
Pods are temporary (ephemeral).
Events that change pod IP:
•	scaling
•	restart
•	node failure
•	new deployment version
Example:
Before restart
Backend Pod → 10.8.0.2
After restart
Backend Pod → 10.8.0.5
Clients would fail if they connect directly to pod IP.
Solution:
Client → Service → Pod
________________________________________
4. Kubernetes Service Types
OpenShift provides five service types.
________________________________________
4.1 ClusterIP (Default)
This service is internal only.
Accessible from:
Pods inside cluster
Example:
Service IP → 172.30.108.92
Used for:
•	backend APIs
•	databases
•	microservices
________________________________________
4.2 LoadBalancer
Creates an external load balancer from a cloud provider.
Example:
Internet → Cloud Load Balancer → Service → Pods
Use case:
•	public applications
•	production environments
Warning:
Cloud providers may charge for load balancers.
________________________________________
4.3 NodePort
Exposes service on node IP + port.
Port range:
30000 – 32767
Example:
Node IP: 192.168.1.20
Port: 30080
Access:
http://192.168.1.20:30080
Security concern:
Direct node access increases attack surface.
________________________________________
4.4 ExternalIP
Maps a cluster node virtual IP to a service.
Example:
External IP → Node → Pod
Important:
Starting with OpenShift 4.18, this is considered legacy.
Reasons:
•	security risks
•	direct node exposure
________________________________________
4.5 ExternalName
Maps Kubernetes service to an external DNS name.
Example:
Service → external database
Example configuration:
externalName: database.example.com
DNS returns a CNAME record.
________________________________________
5. Exposing Applications Outside the Cluster
Kubernetes supports two main methods.
________________________________________
Method 1 — OpenShift Routes (Preferred)
OpenShift provides:
•	OpenShift Routes
Routes expose applications using hostnames.
Example:
https://api.apps.example.com
Routes are handled by the:
•	OpenShift Ingress Controller
The controller uses HAProxy to route traffic.
________________________________________
Route Architecture
Internet
   |
Ingress Controller
   |
Route Hostname
   |
Service
   |
Pods
________________________________________
Creating a Route
Command:
oc expose service api-frontend --hostname api.apps.acme.com
Explanation:
Parameter	Meaning
service	application service
hostname	public URL
________________________________________
Auto-generated Hostnames
If hostname not specified:
<route-name>-<project-name>.<default-domain>
Example:
frontend-api.apps.example.com
________________________________________
Route Configuration Example
kind: Route
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
metadata:
  name: a-simple-route
spec:
  host: api.apps.acme.com
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: api-frontend
  port:
    targetPort: 8443
Important fields:
Field	Description
name	route name
host	public hostname
service	backend service
targetPort	service port
________________________________________
Delete a Route
oc delete route a-simple-route
________________________________________
6. Kubernetes Ingress
Standard Kubernetes method for exposing services.
Resource:
•	Kubernetes Ingress
Ingress also provides:
•	HTTP routing
•	TLS support
•	path routing
________________________________________
Ingress Example
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: frontend
spec:
  rules:
  - host: www.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        backend:
          service:
            name: frontend
            port:
              number: 80
________________________________________
Ingress with TLS
Example TLS configuration:
tls:
- hosts:
  - www.example.com
  secretName: example-com-tls
TLS secret stores the SSL certificate.
________________________________________
Ingress Command
Create ingress:
oc create ingress ingr-sakila \
--rule="ingr-sakila.apps.ocp4.example.com/*=sakila-service:8080"
Delete ingress:
oc delete ingress frontend
________________________________________
7. Sticky Sessions
Some applications require the same user to reach the same pod.
Example:
User login sessions
shopping carts
This feature is called:
Session Persistence
OpenShift implements it using cookies.
________________________________________
Configure Sticky Sessions (Ingress)
oc annotate ingress ingr-example ingress.kubernetes.io/affinity=cookie
________________________________________
Configure Sticky Sessions (Route)
oc annotate route route-example router.openshift.io/cookie_name=myapp
________________________________________
Test Sticky Session
Capture route hostname:
ROUTE_NAME=$(oc get route route-example -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
Send request and save cookie:
curl $ROUTE_NAME -k -c /tmp/cookie_jar
Reuse cookie:
curl $ROUTE_NAME -k -b /tmp/cookie_jar
________________________________________
8. Scaling Applications
You can increase or decrease the number of pods.
Command:
oc scale --replicas=5 deployment/scale
Result:
ReplicaSet creates 5 pods
Do not modify ReplicaSets manually.
Deployment controller manages them.
________________________________________
9. Load Balancing
Kubernetes services automatically distribute traffic.
Example:
Service
 /   |   \
Pod1 Pod2 Pod3
The service load balances requests.
External traffic is handled by the:
•	OpenShift Ingress Controller
________________________________________
Complete Traffic Flow
External request flow:
User
 |
DNS
 |
Ingress Controller
 |
Route / Ingress
 |
Service
 |
Pods
________________________________________
Important Commands for Exams
Create service
oc expose deployment myapp
Create route
oc expose service myapp
Scale deployment
oc scale --replicas=3 deployment/myapp
View routes
oc get routes
View ingress
oc get ingress
